## Pipeline (final machine — keep this order in narrative)

1. Cube scrambled (web/iPad UI — manual or random)
2. Two cameras capture feeds → OpenCV (Python) HSV color detect on 54 cublets
3. Normalize to **URFDLB** state string
4. **Kociemba** computes move sequence
5. Sequence → Arduino MEGA (serial **or** network / ESP WiFi webserver)
6. Six NEMA 17 steppers (A4988 drivers) execute 90° face turns
7. Solve time cited: **~1–2 s**; paper highlights **~1.5 s**; PPT shows **1.8 s / 1.5 s**; wired UI example **1.574 s** on `python-vision.png`

Hardware gist: 3D-printed globe frame, 6 steppers + A4988, Arduino MEGA WiFi (RobotDyn / ESP), ATX 200W PSU, dual cameras + LED strips for even light, PC + iPad control.

Software stack (four pieces in the paper):

1. Color detection + Kociemba (Python / OpenCV)
2. Motor control (C on Arduino)
3. ESP webserver hosting control app
4. Web UI (JS/HTML/CSS — adapted from Google Chrome Cube Lab) for 3D cube + scramble/solve UX

## Chronological line (canonical)

Use this arc for chapters / Evolution; prefer *thinking* beats over title lists. Subject = Isaque / the machine.

| Phase | When | What happened |
|-------|------|----------------|
| **Challenge** | Pre-build / course | Takes on building a machine that solves any 3×3 scramble |
| **Research** | Early 2019 | Study similar robots; Flatland (2016) world-record ~0.9 s as structural reference |
| **Electronics v0** | Early–mid | Single NEMA 17 + A4988 proof → six-motor board; ATX 200W replaces weak PSU; Arduino MEGA WiFi |
| **Prototype 1 (Flatland-like)** | Spring 2019 | Corel → DXF → Fusion 360 → FFF on Faculdade Única printer; reading base for Intelbras VIP 1120 cam; face-by-face / fixed-base read |
| **Robotics & AI fair** | **June 2019** | First **functional** prototype on show; stand + visitors; strong reception; spans almost the whole CS curriculum in one build |
| **Diagnosis** | Post-fair | Flatland-like frame: multi-camera burden, print warp / low print quality limits |
| **Magic Globe redesign** | Mid–late 2019 | New globe geometry: 6 motors centered on faces, sightlines for **two** cameras / all 54 stickers; stronger prints; LED rings for uniform illumination (shadow fix) |
| **Full stack** | Concurrent | OpenCV+Kociemba · Arduino motion · ESP web · iPad/web UI; scramble→read→solve loop |
| **Results** | Late 2019 | Sub-2 s solves; ~1.5 s cited as achieved mark; cost table for **final** build only (~**R$ 5.000** in PPT — ignore discarded prototype spend) |
| **Defense** | **10 Dec 2019** | TCC approved — B.Sc. Computer Science, Faculdade Única de Ipatinga |
| **Afterlife** | Post-TCC | Open-source / portfolio embed; local media versions **v1.0 → v2.0 → v2.1**; optional future: quick-release motors for physical scramble |

